+**Outside checks.** nl-shell also reads, from outside bash, the PTY's terminal settings
+(`tcgetattr` on the master) and what bash's fds 0, 1 and 2 are (`/proc/<pid>/fd` and
+`fdinfo`: the file and its access mode). It reads them twice per command:
+
+1. At the end of the hooks. After `E` the hook blocks reading fd 4; nl-shell waits until
+   `/proc/<pid>/syscall` shows bash itself blocked in `read` on fd 4 and fd 4 is its pipe,
+   takes the readings, and writes one line to fd 4 only if they equal the readings taken
+   at this point at the first prompt. Nothing but nl-shell's own hook code runs between
+   reading `H` and this point, and readline has not yet taken the terminal for the next
+   line, so this sees the settings bash itself has at the end of its hooks: the ones
+   readline saves and restores around the next line, so the next command runs with them
+   unless something changes them after this reading. It is the only reading that can see
+   `-isig`, `-icanon` or `-echo`.
+2. Just before every paste, while readline holds the terminal (bash blocked as in §4.2),
+   compared with a reading taken in the same state at the first prompt. Readline turns
+   `ISIG` on and `ICANON` and `ECHO` off while it holds the terminal, so this reading
+   cannot see bash's own values of those; it sees the settings readline does not reset
+   (`istrip`, `icrnl`, `inlcr`, the `intr` character…) and a change a background job
+   made while readline held the terminal.
+
+Any difference degrades the session and nothing is written. A change made between the
+second reading and the write is a known gap (#147). These checks need Linux `/proc`.
 
